// `std::watch` — the process layer's dev-mode surface (`dev-refresh.md` §5
// item 2). Named apart from the browser's `std::dev` (`std/src/browser/dev.vl`)
// on purpose: `std_twin_parity.rs`'s `TWINNED_MODULES` documents `dev` as
// deliberately single-platform (browser-only, no counterpart to hold it
// against — the same reasoning `db`/`fs`/`http`/`process`/`rpc_server` already
// get on the process side), so reusing that name here would silently open a
// twin the parity gate was never meant to cover — the two surfaces share no
// component source the way `std::ui`'s two halves do, which is what that gate
// actually protects.
//
// `force_refresh()` is the manual half of the ruling's composed mechanism:
// check `is_watching()`, re-read cheaply, `force_refresh()`, and the browser
// re-pulls current bytes over the existing push routes. A no-op outside
// `vilan run --watch` — importing this module costs nothing in a shipped
// process.
//
import pkg::fetch::post;
import pkg::option::Option::{ None, Some, self };
import pkg::process::env;

/// Whether this process is the child of a `vilan run --watch` session — the dev
/// loop (`dev-refresh.md` §5, item 2).
///
/// Uniform: DEFINED under every run and `false` outside a watch, so a program
/// branches on it without knowing how it was started. `std::dev` is the
/// browser's answer to the same question and is browser-only by construction
/// (HMR swaps the browser leg in place; the node leg restarts), so this is the
/// process layer's own.
///
/// What it is for is FRESHNESS, not code: a server that restarts on every code
/// change can still serve week-old bytes for a file it read once at the top of
/// `main`. `serve_build` uses it to revalidate its assets per request while
/// watching and serve them from memory otherwise; a hand-rolled server can do
/// the same. Server-side HMR remains a permanent non-goal (`hmr.md` §8).
///
/// The signal is the `VILAN_WATCHING` environment variable the watcher sets on
/// the Node child it spawns — nothing that can fail independently of the
/// process actually starting, and nothing left behind by a watcher that died.
fun is_watching(): bool {
	match env("VILAN_WATCHING") {
		Some(let value) => value == "1",
		None => false,
	}
}

/// Ask every connected browser to reload once (`dev-refresh.md` §5 item 2).
/// The watcher hands its child the dev channel's port over `VILAN_HMR_PORT`
/// and that run's channel token over `VILAN_HMR_TOKEN` (the node-child spawn
/// site inside `run --watch`'s HMR round); with no watch session — the env
/// vars unset, a plain `vilan run`, or any shipped process — this is a no-op.
/// Fire-and-forget: the call doesn't wait for the POST to land or for the
/// browser to actually reload, so it never blocks the caller.
///
/// The token is required by every dev-channel route (backlog E93): the channel
/// used to take an unauthenticated `POST /refresh`, which meant any page the
/// developer's browser happened to be on could fire a reload flood at the dev
/// session. This process is the one legitimate caller that is not the browser
/// shim, and it learns the token the same way it learns everything else about
/// the session — an environment variable inherited at spawn, alongside
/// `VILAN_WATCHING`. `zip` is the "both or nothing" the pairing wants: a port
/// without a token could only produce a refused POST, so it stays a no-op.
fun force_refresh(): void {
	match env("VILAN_HMR_PORT").zip(env("VILAN_HMR_TOKEN")) {
		Some(let (port, token)) => {
			let _ = async post(i"http://127.0.0.1:{port}/refresh?token={token}", "").send();
		},
		None => {},
	}
}
